#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:0
msgid ""
"Understand how confdb schema assertions separate configuration data from "
"snap installation, enabling cross-snap settings, access control, and device "
"onboarding."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:8
msgid "Confdb configuration mechanism"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:10
msgid ""
"*Confdb* is an assertion-backed alternative to individual {ref}`snap-"
"specific configuration <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-set-system-options>`. It "
"uses an external [confdb-schema](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/core/"
"reference/assertions/confdb-schema/) assertion to separate the configuration "
"data from the snap installation. This allows for advanced configuration "
"functionality, including:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:12
msgid "device onboarding and management"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:13
msgid "cross-snap configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:14
msgid "precise access control"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:15
msgid "evolving configuration settings without changing the affected snaps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:17
msgid ""
"See {ref}`How to configure snaps with confdb <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-"
"configure-snaps-with-confdb>` for instructions on using confdb with your own "
"snaps, and read on for specific implementation details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:19
msgid ""
"Confdb is currently considered an {ref}`Experimental feature <ref-"
"experimental-features_experimental-features>` and implementation details may "
"change as development progresses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:22
msgid "Confdb views"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:24
msgid ""
"Settings are exposed via _views_. Views allow for both granular access "
"control and *granular configuration contracts* to enable the evolution of a "
"configuration value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:26
msgid ""
"![Architectural overview, showing users and snaps interacting with separate "
"confdb databags through granular views](/images/confdb-architecture.png) "
"**Figure: Architectural overview, showing users and snaps interacting with "
"separate confdb databags through granular views.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:26
msgid ""
"Architectural overview, showing users and snaps interacting with separate "
"confdb databags through granular views"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:29
msgid ""
"The `views` header in the [confdb-schema assertion](https://"
"documentation.ubuntu.com/core/reference/assertions/confdb-schema/#confdb-"
"schema-assertion-fields) contains sets of rules exposing a \"view\" onto the "
"configuration space. Each view rule map a `request` path to a `storage` path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:31
msgid ""
"Users and snaps can access the _request_ path, while the _storage_ path "
"defines the layout in storage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:33
msgid ""
"The separation of the *request* path from the *storage* path provides the "
"isolation, allowing the configuration layout to evolve without having to "
"propagate those changes to every accessing snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:35
msgid "Confdb view requests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:37
msgid ""
"When making a request, an administrator (or a snap accessing a "
"configuration) must specify a view. A request path is required for writes "
"and optional for reads; if the request path is omitted on a read, the "
"request is matched against all readable rules in the view."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:39
msgid ""
"A rule matches when the supplied request is equal to, or is a prefix of, the "
"rule's request path. The corresponding storage path is then used to retrieve "
"a value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:41
msgid ""
"All matching readable rules are evaluated. If more than one rule matches the "
"request, the result is aggregated from all of the values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:43
msgid ""
"Multiple views can have the same “request” field, but different “storage”, "
"facilitating backwards compatibility when transitioning to a new layout."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:45
msgid "Example implementation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:47
msgid ""
"The following is almost the simplest confdb-schema assertion that could be "
"defined to obtain some information about installed snaps, although we omit "
"the other headers and the body for brevity. See {ref}`Configure snaps with "
"confdb <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-configure-snaps-with-confdb>` for the "
"full details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:50
msgid "Basic example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:52
msgid ""
"type: confdb-schema\n"
"account-id: system\n"
"name: snaps\n"
"\n"
"[...]\n"
"\n"
"views:\n"
"  state:\n"
"    rules:\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: snaps.firefox.status\n"
"        storage: v1.snaps.firefox.status\n"
"        access: read\n"
"\n"
"[...]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:70
msgid "This above configuration could be accessed as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:72
msgid ""
"$ snap get system/snaps/state snaps.firefox.status\n"
"enabled\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:77
msgid ""
"We now want to expand the above example to allow status and *revision* "
"queries for many different snaps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:79
msgid "Placeholders"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:81
msgid ""
"Instead of enumerating every possible combination, we can use a placeholder "
"to map a variable in the request path to the storage path:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:83
msgid ""
"[...]\n"
"    rules:\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: snaps.{name}.status\n"
"        storage: v1.snaps.{name}.status\n"
"        access: read\n"
"[...]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:93
msgid "This enables the snap name to be dynamically mapped into the query:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:95
msgid ""
"$ snap get system/snaps/state snaps.firefox.status\n"
"enabled\n"
"\n"
"$ snap get system/snaps/state snaps.spotify.status\n"
"disabled\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:103
msgid "Nested paths"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:105
msgid ""
"To add more configuration paths for snaps, either add more rules or, if "
"possible, use a “content” shorthand to define nested paths:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:107
msgid ""
"[...]\n"
"\n"
"  state:\n"
"    rules:\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: snaps.{name}\n"
"        storage: v1.snaps.{name}\n"
"        access: read\n"
"        content:\n"
"          - storage: status\n"
"          - storage: revision\n"
"\n"
"[...]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:123
msgid ""
"This example only provides a storage path. When omitted, the request path is "
"assumed to be equal to the storage path by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:125
msgid "Aggregate results"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:127
msgid ""
"As requests are matched on *prefixes* of the request paths in view rules, "
"it’s possible to provide a prefix path to \\`snap get\\` and to obtain an "
"aggregate result:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:129
msgid ""
"$ snap get system/snaps/state snaps.firefox.status\n"
"enabled\n"
"\n"
"$ snap get system/snaps/state snaps.spotify.revision\n"
"7\n"
"\n"
"$ snap get system/snaps/state snaps\n"
"{\n"
"  \"firefox\": {\n"
"    \"status\": \"enabled\"\n"
"  },\n"
"  \"spotify\": {\n"
"    \"status\": \"disabled\",\n"
"    \"revision\": 7\n"
"  }\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:148
msgid "Evolving configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:150
msgid ""
"All the rule mappings defined above preserve the same structure after adding "
"the `v1` storage prefix. As snapd maps request paths to storage paths, let’s "
"say a user or snap is interested in knowing the system’s (in this case, "
"snapd’s) status. To take advantage of confdb’s features for evolving "
"configuration schemas and re-organising namespaces, we could add the "
"following rule:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:152
msgid ""
"...\n"
"    rules:\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: snaps.{name}\n"
"        storage: v1.snaps.{name}\n"
"        access: read\n"
"        content:\n"
"          - storage: status\n"
"          - storage: revision\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: system-status\n"
"        storage: v1.snaps.snapd.status\n"
"        access: read\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:168
msgid ""
"This maps an already exposed path (snaps.snapd.status) under a new request "
"path, allowing us to query `system-status` directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:170
msgid ""
"In the future, if a new path was intended to provide the value for `system-"
"status`, we would only need to modify the mapping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:172
msgid ""
"$ snap get system/snaps/state\n"
"{\n"
"  \"snaps\": {\n"
"    \"firefox\": {\n"
"      \"status\": \"enabled\"\n"
"    },\n"
"    \"spotify\": {\n"
"      \"status\": \"disabled\",\n"
"      \"revision\": 7\n"
"    },\n"
"    \"snapd\": {\n"
"      \"status\": \"enabled\"\n"
"    },\n"
"  },\n"
"  \"system-status\": \"enabled\"\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:191
msgid "Namespaces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:193
msgid ""
"If the configuration data layout changes, only the assertion needs to be "
"modified. The snap sees the same namespace, isolating it from schema "
"changes. For example, the following assertion exposes the same data under "
"the same namespace (from a user or snap’s perspective) but the storage "
"layout it assumes is completely different:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:195
msgid ""
"...\n"
"    rules:\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: snaps.{name}.status\n"
"        storage: v1.statuses.{name}\n"
"        access: read\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: snaps.{name}.revision\n"
"        storage: v1.revisions.{name}\n"
"        access: read\n"
"      -\n"
"        request: system-status\n"
"        storage: v1.statuses.snapd\n"
"        access: read\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:213
msgid ""
"The configuration namespace should be considered carefully up front. While "
"new confdb views can be added and existing ones can be extended, devices in "
"the field may be running older snap versions that still depend on the "
"existing namespace and views. Removing or renaming request paths, views, or "
"schemas can therefore break those older snaps, making \"outdated\" "
"namespaces difficult to retire safely."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:217
msgid "Interface Plugs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:219
msgid ""
"Snaps can use the {ref}`confdb interface <interfaces-confdb>` to express "
"their interest in accessing a view through the plug stanza in their "
"snapcraft.yaml (then snap.yaml). For example, a snap wishing to access the "
"same `state` view would declare it as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:221
msgid ""
"plugs:\n"
"  snaps-state:\n"
"    interface: confdb\n"
"    account: system\n"
"    view: snaps/state\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:229
msgid ""
"If the snap publisher signed the confdb assertion, the confdb interface will "
"be auto-connected. Otherwise, the plug must be connected to the `:confdb` "
"slot manually."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:231
msgid ""
"The snap can then refer to this interface plug when accessing confdb with "
"the `snapctl` commands. It must also use the `--view` flag to signal this as "
"a confdb request:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:233
msgid ""
"$ snapctl get --view :snaps-state snaps.firefox.status\n"
"enabled\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:238
msgid ""
"Confdb supports the same commands and options as the {ref}`original "
"configuration mechanism <how-to-guides-work-with-snaps-configure-snaps>`. "
"This includes unsetting data using `snapctl unset --view ...` or by using "
"`snapctl set --view` with `!` suffixed to the request path, for example "
"`snapctl set --view :snaps-state snaps.firefox.status!`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:240
msgid "Custodians"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:242
msgid ""
"The confdb interface plug can have one other attribute, `role`, which is "
"optional and can only take a value of `custodian`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:244
msgid ""
"`custodian` declares that the snap has a special role in managing the data "
"accessed through the referenced view. This applies to an exact account, "
"schema, and view combination, so a custodian for one view does not satisfy "
"the requirement for another view in the same schema. For every non-system "
"confdb view, at least one custodian must be installed and connected for the "
"data to be accessible. Snaps cannot be custodians of system views, but they "
"can still define `observe-view-<plug>` hooks on those views. See the [Hooks "
"section](#hooks) for further details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:246
msgid ""
"One of the key aspects of the custodian role relates to ephemeral data which "
"we’ll cover in the next section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:248
msgid "Ephemeral data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:250
msgid ""
"In the context of confdb, ephemeral data is configuration data for which the "
"authoritative version is stored outside of snapd. Snapd may retain data, but "
"this should only be considered a cache, while the custodians are responsible "
"for loading and storing the authoritative version during read and write "
"accesses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:252
msgid ""
"Configuration paths are marked as ephemeral in the storage schema which is "
"specified in the body of the [confdb-schema assertion](https://"
"documentation.ubuntu.com/core/reference/assertions/confdb-schema/#ephemeral-"
"data)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:254
msgid ""
"During `snap` or `snapctl` access (whether to read or write), changes are "
"either committed or read as a transaction. Snapd will get data from its "
"storage and then schedule certain [hooks](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/"
"snapcraft/stable/reference/hooks/) from snaps with plugs referring to views "
"affected by the data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:256
msgid ""
"We’ll cover hooks in more detail in the next section so for now we’ll just "
"focus on two: `save-view-<plug>` and `load-view-<plug>`. These are invoked "
"for custodian snaps when writing and reading, respectively, and are suffixed "
"with the name of the interface plug for which the snap is custodian."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:258
msgid ""
"When snapd detects that a `snap` or `snapctl set` might impact ephemeral "
"data, it stores the changes in a transaction object and invokes `save-view-"
"<plug>` hooks for connected custodians. These hooks can then query the "
"ongoing transaction with the usual `snapctl get --view ...` commands to read "
"changes to the ephemeral data and then store them off-snapd as they wish. "
"Snapd will also store these changes but it does so as a cache, not as an "
"authoritative version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:260
msgid ""
"When ephemeral data is read, snapd will similarly schedule `load-view-"
"<plug>` hooks that give custodians an opportunity to read new data outside "
"of snapd and then load into the transaction read with the usual `snapctl set "
"--view ...` commands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:263
msgid "Hooks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:265
msgid ""
"Confdb supports 5 types of [hooks](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/"
"snapcraft/stable/reference/hooks/):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:267
msgid "`load-view-<plug>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:268
msgid "`query-view-<plug>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:269
msgid "`change-view-<plug>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:270
msgid "`save-view-<plug>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:271
msgid "`observe-view-<plug>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:273
msgid ""
"`load-view-<plug>` and `query-view-<plug>` are scheduled during read "
"operations, while `change-view-<plug>`, `save-view-<plug>` and `observe-view-"
"<plug>` are relevant only to writes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:275
msgid ""
"The `load-view-<plug>` and `save-view-<plug>` hooks are optional unless the "
"user can access data marked as `ephemeral`. Accessed paths are checked at "
"run-time against the storage schema for any `ephemeral` schema types along "
"or under the path. If ephemeral data may be impacted and the appropriate "
"hooks are not defined for at least one connected custodian then the access "
"is rejected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:277
msgid "During a read of the confdb data:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:278
msgid ""
"snapd first reads the configuration data it stores internally and creates a "
"transaction object to carry it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:279
msgid ""
"Snapd then schedules `load-view-<plug>` hooks for any custodian snaps with "
"interface plugs for the referenced view. These hooks allow the snaps to load "
"the ephemeral data, if they wish to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:280
msgid ""
"After those hooks load any off-loaded data, the `query-view-<plug>` hooks "
"run for any relevant custodian that defines them. These hooks give the "
"custodian snaps a chance to modify the read data before it’s returned to the "
"reader. This could be used to apply a constraint that couldn’t be enforced "
"on write (e.g., if the data is ephemeral and could be modified off-snapd)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:282
msgid ""
"Both series of hooks are run in the same order, sorted by their snap’s name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:284
msgid "Writes to confdb data operate in a similar fashion to reads:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:285
msgid ""
"Snapd creates a transaction object, storing the intended changes to the "
"confdb data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:286
msgid ""
"The first hooks to be scheduled are the `change-view-<plug>` hooks. These "
"hooks provide an opportunity for custodian snaps to modify the data before "
"it’s committed or even to reject it entirely. This is useful if the snaps "
"must maintain complex invariants that can’t be expressed as [schema types "
"and constraints](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/core/reference/assertions/"
"confdb-schema/#schema-types)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:287
msgid ""
"Data modification is done with `snapctl set --view` command in the usual "
"manner. To help snaps distinguish between uncommitted and pristine data, a "
"`--previous` flag can be used with `snapctl get --view`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:290
msgid ""
"$ snapctl get --view :plug-name path.modified\n"
"<new_data>\n"
"\n"
"$ snapctl get --view --previous :plug-name path.modified\n"
"<old_data>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:298
msgid ""
"It’s also possible to reject the change altogether while providing a reason "
"for the rejection:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:300
msgid "$ snapctl fail <reason>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:304
msgid ""
"Once all `change-view-<plug>` hooks have run, the `save-view-<plug>` hooks "
"run in the same order (i.e., ordered by snap name). If a `save-view-<plug>` "
"hook fails, the transaction is cancelled. The failing hook and any "
"previously completed `save-view-<plug>` hooks are invoked again with "
"pristine data, in reverse invocation order, to help custodian snaps maintain "
"consistency among the various versions of the configuration data. Errors "
"from these rollback runs are ignored, and the original failure is reported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-snaps-work/confdb-configuration-mechanism.md:306
msgid ""
"After all `save-view-<plug>` hooks succeed, snapd commits the transaction "
"and then schedules `observe-view-<plug>` hooks. This is the only type of "
"confdb hook scheduled for non-custodian plugs, although custodian plugs can "
"also receive it. The hook notifies snaps plugging views affected by the "
"write that configuration data relevant to them may have changed. The snap "
"that initiated the write does not receive its own observe hook. Errors from "
"all observe hooks are ignored and cannot roll back the already committed "
"transaction."
msgstr ""
